{"name":"Orange Roughy","hex":"#a85335","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"orange roughy","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An edible deep-sea fish (Hoplostethus atlanticus), of the slimehead family (Trachichthyidae)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/orange_roughy"}],"entries":[{"title":"Orange roughy","blocks":[{"text":"The orange roughy, also known as the red roughy, slimehead and deep sea perch, is a relatively large deep-sea fish belonging to the slimehead family (Trachichthyidae). It is bathypelagic, found in cold, deep waters of the Western Pacific Ocean, eastern Atlantic Ocean, Indo-Pacific, and in the eastern Pacific off Chile. The orange roughy is notable for its extraordinary lifespan, attaining over 200 years. The fish has a bright, brick red color, fading to a yellowish-orange after death."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Orange_roughy.png/330px-Orange_roughy.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orange_roughy","wikidata":"Q1479333"},{"title":"Slimehead","blocks":[{"text":"Slimeheads, also known as roughies and redfish, are mostly small, exceptionally long-lived, deep-sea beryciform fish constituting the family Trachichthyidae, coming from the Ancient Greek τραχύς (trakhús), meaning \"rough\", and ἰχθύς (ikhthús), meaning \"fish\". Found in temperate to tropical waters of the Atlantic, Indian, and Pacific Oceans, the family comprises about 50 species in eight genera. Slimeheads are named for the network of muciferous canals riddling their heads."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Orange_roughy.png/330px-Orange_roughy.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slimehead","wikidata":"Q1475478"},{"title":"Orange (colour)","blocks":[{"text":"Orange is the colour between yellow and red on the spectrum of visible light. The human eyes perceive orange when observing light with a dominant wavelength between roughly 585 and 620 nanometres. In traditional colour theory, it is a secondary colour of pigments, produced by mixing yellow and red. In the RGB colour model, it is a tertiary colour. It is named after the fruit of the same name."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Delicatearch.png/330px-Delicatearch.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orange_(colour)","wikidata":"Q39338"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Hoplostethus_atlanticus_NOAA.jpg/500px-Hoplostethus_atlanticus_NOAA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Hoplostethus_atlanticus_NOAA.jpg/1280px-Hoplostethus_atlanticus_NOAA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Hoplostethus_atlanticus_NOAA.jpg/500px-Hoplostethus_atlanticus_NOAA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHoplostethus_atlanticus_NOAA.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Faroe_stamp_251_orange_roughy_%28hoplostethus_atlanticus%29.gif/330px-Faroe_stamp_251_orange_roughy_%28hoplostethus_atlanticus%29.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Faroe_stamp_251_orange_roughy_%28hoplostethus_atlanticus%29.gif/330px-Faroe_stamp_251_orange_roughy_%28hoplostethus_atlanticus%29.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Fish in the Faroe Islands:Orange roughy, Hoplostethus atlanticusFaroese stamp issued: 7 Feb 1994Artist: Astrid Andreasen","link":"https: